# a11y capture: yt-comments-sheet-scroll
# device: Pixel 3 (192.168.1.68:5555), Android 12 / API 31. Captured 2026-09-11.
# HOW: YouTube force-stopped, relaunched, its 3-second DELAY overlay left to auto-complete
#      untouched, then the comments of a feed item opened by hand, then EXACTLY THREE slow scroll
#      gestures inside the comments list, 1.5s apart. Driven by the device-tester agent; YouTube
#      rather than Instagram because Instagram and TikTok are never driven over adb
#      (tools/qa/ig-walkthrough-capture.sh).
# EXPECTED (test oracle): 0 while a feed is the established primary scroll source (21 events) — scrolling a
#      sheet opened over a feed is not content consumed from the feed, and the overlay calling it
#      "reels" is what the report objects to. Replayed on its OWN, with nothing else ever having
#      scrolled, this list IS what the user is consuming and scores 8 (the number of comments they
#      actually moved past), NOT 20 (the number of events).
# WHAT IT PROVES: three human gestures produced TWENTY-ONE TYPE_VIEW_SCROLLED events. That is issue
#      #28's "scrolling the comments counts ~10 taps continuously", measured: the old event-rate
#      rule turns three gestures into roughly one count per 500ms of gesture. fromIndex across the
#      stream reads 2,3,4,4,4,4,4,5,6,7,7,7,7,8,9,10,10,10,10,10 — eight forward transitions.
# CAVEAT, recorded honestly: no counter RULE was active on this surface (the whole-app DELAY rule
#      did not apply once feature=SHORTS was detected), so this capture cannot show what Nudge's
#      counter DID — only what the event stream was. The counting is asserted by replay instead.
# CAVEAT 2: this stream contains ONE scroll source. The feed-vs-sheet exclusion is therefore
#      exercised in the replay test by priming a feed source first, not by the capture itself —
#      because YouTube's own feed emits no scroll events at all on this build (see docs/BACKLOG.md).
